test_arima_surface: mojolearn.ARIMA, tier fast, n_obs 512

THIS PROCESS LOADED THE FAST BUILD, WHICH MAKES NO CROSS-VENDOR
CLAIM. The bitwise arms below are RECORDED and NOT ASSERTED, and
each one says so on its own line. What this run does check is
real: the marshalling, the element counts, the packing, every
refusal, the planted-parameter recovery and the closed-form
forecast. It checks NO BIT. For the identity half:

  MOJOLEARN_NUMERIC_MODE=identical \
      python3 -m mojolearn.tests.test_arima_surface

  PROVENANCE (990)
    pass the estimator's tier is the process's tier
    pass the loaded binary is not the identical one
    pass the binary exports arima_vendor for _backend.py's cross-check
    pass the binary exports arima_numeric_mode for the tier read-back

  REFUSALS, python side (992)
    pass trend='t' is refused by name
    pass trend='ct' is refused by name
    pass a polynomial trend specification is refused by name
    pass an unspelled method is refused
    pass verbose is refused, it selects log lines this port never prints
    pass output_type is refused, this package returns NumPy
    pass a 2-tuple order is refused
    pass a negative order entry is refused
    pass a 3-tuple seasonal_order is refused
    pass level is NOT A PARAMETER of this class, so it is a TypeError; cuML's confidence intervals are unported
    pass simple_differencing is NOT A PARAMETER of this class; only the True arm exists in this lane
    pass start_params is NOT A PARAMETER; every fit starts from estimate_x0
    pass a 3-D y is refused
    pass predict before fit is refused
    pass forecast before fit is refused

  REFUSALS, mojo side (992)
    pass exog is refused by validate_order, as n_exog != 0
    pass method='css' is refused by arima/estimator.mojo, only MLE is offered
    pass method='css-ml' is refused by the same file
    pass p > 8 is refused by validate_order, in cuML's words
    pass d + D > 2 is refused by validate_order
    pass an order with no parameters at all is refused
    pass a seasonal term with s < 2 is refused
    pass rd > 8 is refused; it selects cuML's block-per-series Kalman kernel, which is unported
    pass r > 5 is refused; it selects cuML's Schur/Sylvester Lyapunov solver, which is unported
    pass a NaN is refused by name and by INDEX; missing observations are a cuML path with no port, so a NaN here is not missing data, it is refused input
    pass a one-observation series is refused

  REFUSALS, after a fit (992)
    pass predict with end <= start is refused, and the message names BOTH conventions
    pass forecast(0) is refused
    pass mu_ raises when trend resolved to 'n', rather than returning a zero-width mean

  RECOVERY
    pass ar1: params_ is (batch, N) with N = p + q + k + 1
    pass ar1: the per-series arrays are batch-long
    pass ar1: phi recovered on all 6 series, worst |error| 0.06917 within 4.75 standard errors (se 0.03156, bound 0.14991)
    pass ar1: the 6 series produced 6 DISTINCT phi, so nothing was broadcast
    pass ar1: every series reported OPT_SUCCESS
    pass ar1: llf_ agrees with -fx_ * (n_obs - 1) to 3.75e-08, so the second Kalman pass evaluated the fit's own point
    pass ma1: params_ is (batch, N) with N = p + q + k + 1
    pass ma1: the per-series arrays are batch-long
    pass ma1: theta recovered on all 6 series, worst |error| 0.06676 within 5.23 standard errors (se 0.03827, bound 0.20017)
    pass ma1: the 6 series produced 6 DISTINCT theta, so nothing was broadcast
    pass ma1: every series reported OPT_SUCCESS
    pass ma1: llf_ agrees with -fx_ * (n_obs - 1) to 3.03e-08, so the second Kalman pass evaluated the fit's own point
    pass arma11: params_ is (batch, N) with N = p + q + k + 1
    pass arma11: the per-series arrays are batch-long
    pass arma11: phi recovered on all 6 series, worst |error| 0.08834 within 7.07 standard errors (se 0.03536, bound 0.24996)
    pass arma11: the 6 series produced 6 DISTINCT phi, so nothing was broadcast
    pass arma11: theta recovered on all 6 series, worst |error| 0.11566 within 5.93 standard errors (se 0.04216, bound 0.25000)
    pass arma11: the 6 series produced 6 DISTINCT theta, so nothing was broadcast
    pass arma11: every series reported OPT_SUCCESS
    pass arma11: llf_ agrees with -fx_ * (n_obs - 1) to 3.86e-08, so the second Kalman pass evaluated the fit's own point

  CRITERIA (991)
    pass ar1: aic_ == 2N - 2 llf with N = 2
    pass ar1: bic_ == log(T) N - 2 llf with T = 512, the count AFTER differencing
    pass ar1: the BIC penalty exceeds the AIC penalty at n = 512, since log(T) > 2
    pass ma1: aic_ == 2N - 2 llf with N = 2
    pass ma1: bic_ == log(T) N - 2 llf with T = 512, the count AFTER differencing
    pass ma1: the BIC penalty exceeds the AIC penalty at n = 512, since log(T) > 2
    pass arma11: aic_ == 2N - 2 llf with N = 3
    pass arma11: bic_ == log(T) N - 2 llf with T = 512, the count AFTER differencing
    pass arma11: the BIC penalty exceeds the AIC penalty at n = 512, since log(T) > 2

  PREDICT
    pass predict(0, n_obs) is (batch, n_obs); `end` IS EXCLUDED, which is cuML's convention and not statsmodels'
    pass end=None means n_obs, as upstream
    pass predict(3, 9) is six wide, not seven
    pass with d = 0 no in-sample prediction is NaN
    pass a windowed predict is the same bits as the slice of the full one  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass the d = 1 model predicts (batch, n_obs) too
    pass step 0 is NaN when d = 1: differencing consumes it, and the sentinel is the canonical 0x7fc00000 by constant (DEVIATION 676)
    pass everything from step d onward is finite
    pass the undefined prediction is the CANONICAL quiet NaN 0x7fc00000 on every vendor, not the hardware's own payload  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass the (1,1,1) model with trend='c' packs mu, ar, ma, sigma2
    pass mu_ exists and is batch-long when trend='c'

  FORECAST
    pass forecast(6) is (batch, 6)
    pass no forecast step is NaN, there is no in-sample block in one
    pass the AR(1) forecast is phi^h times the last observation to 1.85e-07 relative, in closed form, against a reference no kernel produced
    pass a stationary AR(1) forecast decays; |step 6| does not exceed |step 1|
    pass forecast(h) is predict(n_obs, n_obs + h), bit for bit  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass the d = 1 model forecasts four finite steps
    pass the differenced forecast continues the LEVEL of the series, so step 1 is the same order as the last observation; a missing finalize_forecast would return the differenced scale

  BATCH COMPOSITION
    pass three series drawn from six fit to the same bits  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass and to the same ITERATION COUNTS, which is the stage that catches a per-series branch reading another series' state
    pass and the same retcodes
    pass ONE series fitted alone is the same bits as that series inside a batch of six  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass a batch of one is still 2-D, (1, N)
    pass the forecast is batch-composition invariant too  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass and so is the in-sample prediction  [FAST: not asserted]
    pass a 1-D y is exactly the (1, n_obs) fit, not a different problem  [FAST: not asserted]

  binary  /root/mojolearn/bench/results/e1/2026-09-06_091452-mojolearn-e2-amd/diag/candidate/qualification-normalized/venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/mojolearn/hip/gfx942/_mojolearn_arima.so
